---
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import { pilots } from '../../data/site.js';
---
<!-- Three registers, corner-anchored, with an open photographic band between
     the poster and the ledger:

       1. caption rail   top-right only — the nav holds the top-left corner
       2. the poster     upper-left: a quiet lead-in clause, then the claim
       3. the ledger     the four 2026 partners, NAMED, inside the first screen

     The lower half's job is proof. The four institutions that agreed to run a
     pilot are the only credential this page has, so they are set as the
     second-largest voice in the frame — not as a duration tag. Three ranked
     sizes per cell: brass numeral, partner name, project whisper.

     AND THE PHOTOGRAPH HAS TO CARRY THE FRAME, which is the other half of
     the composition. It was not. Measured on the shipped 1440×900 frame in
     round 3: the only thing in the 258px band between the claim's baseline
     and the ledger rule was a crouching silhouette peaking at L 103 and
     averaging 48, while the empty river to its right averaged 87 and peaked
     far higher. The brightest, highest-contrast area of the first screen was
     blank water and the only human in it was the darkest hole in the frame,
     so a reader finishing the sentence fell into the void beside the subject.
     Two things were wrong and both are fixed here.

     1 · THE CROP. `hero.jpg` is 2800×1400 and the pair — a man reaching down
     to his dog at the water's edge — occupies source x 730–1400, y 865–1390,
     i.e. the bottom-left sixth of the negative. Printed at focal 36%/64%,
     zoom 1 that put them in the last 90px of the frame with the ledger over
     their legs, and gave five sixths of the first screen to open river. The
     frame now crops to the pair (see the Figure call), and it crops to them
     TWICE, because a landscape frame and a portrait one do not hold the same
     subject in the same place:

       ≥ 901px   zoom 1.18 · focal 33% 92%    the pair, lower-left third
       ≤ 900px   zoom 1.16 · focal 34% 135%   the pair, in the open band

     The narrow value is the interesting one, and round 4 re-derived it from
     a constraint the earlier rounds never wrote down. On any viewport under
     2:1 the media box is exactly as tall as the cover-scaled photograph, so
     vertical free space is zero and object-position cannot pan — only
     `transform-origin` can, and it is allowed to sit outside the box. That
     is the lever. But the same lever slides the photograph's own BOTTOM EDGE
     up the frame, and there is a hard ceiling on it:

       the media box is 1.16h tall (h + Figure's 8% parallax overscan) and
       starts at -0.08h, so the print's foot lands at
           -0.08h + 1.16h · (s + f(1-s))       s = 1.02 · zoom, f = focal-y
       and covering the frame needs s + f(1-s) ≥ 1.08/1.16 = 0.931.

     h cancels: ONE number governs every narrow viewport. At zoom 1.16 it caps
     focal-y at 137.7%. The shipped 158% was over it, so the print's foot sat
     at y 807 on a 390×844 phone and at y 980 on a 768×1024 tablet — the last
     36px and 44px of those first screens were Figure's procedural plate, not
     the photograph, with the manifest's own ink band the only thing hiding
     the seam. 135% is inside the cap at every size: measured foot 848.7 on
     the phone, 1029.7 at 768×1024, 1206.7 at 900×1200, 937 at 430×932, 744
     at 360×740, 670 at 375×667 — every one of them clears its frame at rest,
     which is the state the first screen is read in. Under scroll the parallax
     lifts the media by up to 5% of the media box and the foot can run 12–40px
     short again, but by then the hero is leaving and that band sits under the
     manifest's 70% ink over a plate seeded to the photograph's own dark
     water. Buying it back would cost focal-y 135 → 110, which drops the man's
     head to y 459 and puts the manifest across the dog's face. The frame at
     rest wins.

     Figure swaps to focalSm/zoomSm at 720px but the ledger becomes the
     one-row manifest at 900, so the 721–900 band gets its own values through
     a :global rule further down — see THE TABLET BAND.

     WHY 135 AND NOT THE CAP. Lower focal-y sets the pair LOWER, and low is
     what the phone wanted: at 158% the man's cap broke the surface 3px under
     the descender of "today.", so the silhouette read as growing out of the
     word. At 135% his head lands at y 415 — 25px of open water under the
     last line — and the dog's head and the reaching arm, which are the whole
     of the picture, sit at 545–625, wholly inside the band. Horizontal moved
     with it, 31% → 34%: at 31% the dog's hindquarters ran off the right edge
     and the manifest's right column (HEALTHY ATHLETES · CLEAN DATA CENTERS ·
     …) was set over its body. At 34% the dog is whole, the man enters at the
     left the way he does on the desktop, and the right third of the ledger
     band is open water.

     2 · THE GRADE. `/media/hero-graded.jpg` is a dodge-and-burn print of
     `/media/hero.jpg` — the negative stays in the repo and is the fallback in
     the candidate list. Nothing is added to or removed from the photograph;
     three regions get a gamma, in SOURCE coordinates so the print travels
     with every crop, which a CSS layer cannot do:

       POOL  ellipse at (1055, 1035–1075), r 700 × 300/430, γ 0.50, gated to
             pixels already above L 44 so the silhouettes stay black. The last
             of the light collecting on the near water, which is where the
             pair is standing.
       RIM   the same centre, r 430 × 210/300, γ 0.60, gated above L 74 — the
             dog's fur edge and the seam of the water only.
       FALL  everything more than ~1.2 pool-radii out, γ 2.55, plus a second
             γ 1.55 ramp from x 1450 → 2350. The open river goes down. The
             dusk sky and the treeline (y < 500) are exempt: they are the only
             warm thing in the picture and they were never the problem.
       FOOT  y 1130 → 1300, γ 2.30. Both crops land the ledger's first row at
             roughly source y 1160, so the manifest's ground is in the print
             and one number covers a 900px desktop and an 844px phone.

     Measured after, 1440×900, photograph alone with the type hidden:

                            before            after
       subject  x250–700    avg 48 pk 103     avg 68 pk 196
       river    x1000–1440  avg 87 pk 237     avg 44 pk 125

     The contrast is inverted: the eye now lands on the person.

     THE ZONES, after all of that:

       0– 64   THE BAR      Figure's top band. A system floor, not ours.
      64–150   THE FAR BANK the picture's own black. No ink needed.
     150–540   THE CLAIM    .hero__veil, and only .hero__veil.
     540–777   THE SUBJECT  NOTHING. The print carries it.
     777–900   THE LEDGER   the print's foot + .hero__index::before.

     The rule the frame is built on: the sentence gets the quiet band, the
     photograph gets the lit one, and no zone is inked twice. -->
<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-h">
  <!-- Register 1 rides on the figure: the location credit is Figure's own
       object (.label, --fg-meta, brass dot, corner-anchored), the same one
       every other full-bleed scene carries. It used to be re-drawn here as
       .hero__place + .hero__dot, with a pulsing beacon ring nothing else on
       the site has — a second construction of a system object, and a piece of
       animated decoration on a civic institute's front door. -->
  <Figure src={['/media/hero-graded.jpg', '/media/hero.jpg']} alt="" ratio="auto"
          grade={0.34}
          focal="33% 92%"   zoom={1.18}
          focalSm="34% 135%" zoomSm={1.16}
          parallax={5} loading="eager"
          scrim="full" rise={0.19} ink={1.02}
          credit="Greenville, Mississippi" creditPos="top-right" class="hero__bg" />
  <div class="hero__veil" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <!-- Register 2. Two voices inside one sentence: the lead-in clause at the
       page's mid tier, the claim at display scale. Read together they are the
       brief's sentence in full, and the h1 carries the whole of it. -->
  <div class="hero__mid shell">
    <h1 class="hero__h" id="hero-h">
      <span class="bridge bridge--lift hero__kicker">The Lion Forum Institute exists to ensure</span>
      <span class="display hero__poster">the technologies <span class="hero__keep">of tomorrow</span> strengthen American families today.</span>
    </h1>
  </div>

  <!-- Register 3. -->
  <div class="hero__index shell">
    <div class="hero__index-head">
      <span class="eyebrow hero__index-label">Four pilots · 2026</span>
      <a class="cue tap hero__scroll" href="#why">
        <span class="label label--lit">Scroll</span><i class="cue__line" aria-hidden="true"></i>
      </a>
    </div>

    <span class="hero__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    <ul class="hero__cells">
      {pilots.map((p) => (
        <li class="hcell">
          <span class="index hcell__i">{p.index}</span>
          <span class="hcell__name">{p.partner}</span>
          <span class="label hcell__sub">{p.title}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100svh;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .hero__bg { position: absolute; inset: 0; aspect-ratio: auto; z-index: -2; }

  /* ── THE CLAIM BAND, and nothing else ────────────────────────────────
     This element used to paint the whole frame: a full-height linear ramp
     (56 → 28 → 8 → 10 → 44 → 88% ink) plus an 82% radial over the top-left
     quadrant — ON TOP OF Figure's own `auto` scrim, which already floors the
     bar band at 90% and runs a type ramp 46% of the way up the frame. The
     hero was paying for legibility twice, and the photograph paid for both.

     Measured on the shipped frame, median L* per cell, PHOTOGRAPH ALONE vs
     WHAT SHIPPED:

                          photograph          shipped
       y   0– 75  sky      44 → 63             4 → 5      the dusk sky, gone
       y  75–225  treeline  1 →  3             3          (already black)
       y 225–560  water    19 → 62            10 → 51     the claim's ground
       y 600–750  SUBJECT  38 → 55             9 → 40     man + dog + river
       y 750–900  foot      3 → 39             3 → 21     the ledger's ground

     Two things were being thrown away. The top of the frame is a violet dusk
     sky at L* 44–63 — the one thing in the picture that says *evening on the
     Mississippi* — and it was being painted out to L* 4 to protect a bar that
     only covers the first 64px. And the photograph's subject, a man reaching
     down to his dog at the water's edge, stood in a band lit at L* 38–55 and
     was scrimmed to L* 9–32: a silhouette needs a bright ground, and the ink
     that was there to carry a 16px ledger 200px lower took it away.

     So the ink is now placed, once, per zone, and each zone is owned by
     exactly one layer:

       the bar      Figure's top band (the system floor, 90% over the bar)
       the far bank NOTHING. It is the picture's own black.
       THE CLAIM    this element, and only this element
       the subject  NOTHING. The print carries it — see the note at the top.
       the foot     the print's own foot + Figure's ramp at rise={0.19}

     `rise` came down from 0.30 to 0.19 in round 3 and the reason is the whole
     point of that round: 0.30 is 270px of a 900px frame, which put the ramp's
     shoulder at y630 — 147px ABOVE the ledger it exists to carry, and right
     across the dog. The ledger's ground is in the print now (a source-space
     foot burn, which follows the crop) plus .hero__index::before (which is
     anchored to the ledger, not the frame), so Figure's ramp only has to seal
     the bottom edge. Measured after: worst row on the phone 4.97 : 1, worst
     row on the desktop 9.4 : 1, and the dog keeps its light.

     Stops are in PIXELS, not percentages: the type block is anchored to the
     top of the frame by --nav-h and a fixed shelf, so its ground has to be
     anchored the same way or it slides off the words on a taller viewport.
     Figure's foot is a percentage because the ledger is bottom-anchored.

     WEIGHT. 0.46 is measured, not chosen: the brightest patch of river under
     the claim renders at L* 53.5 after the house grade, and cream over it was
     3.5:1 — under the 4.5 the legibility budget asks of large type. 0.46
     lands that patch at L* 41 (4.8:1) and the left of the band at L* 28
     (6.5:1), which also gives the composition its shape: a quiet band for the
     sentence, the lit river left alone above and below it.

     THE RELEASE MOVED UP, 672 → 620, and the phone's 512 → 476. The claim's
     last descender lands at y515 on a 1440 frame and y382 on a 390 one, so
     the old stops were holding 150px and 130px of ink under type that had
     already finished — over the exact band the subject now stands in. The
     stops are set 25px past the last descender and released 80px after that.
     Worst measured contrast on the claim after the move: 5.33 : 1 desktop,
     4.73 : 1 phone, both over the lit water, both over the 4.5 floor. */
  .hero__veil {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1; pointer-events: none;
    /* Stops are named so the narrow viewport can move them: the claim is
       five lines on a phone and three on a desktop, so the band that carries
       it is a different length, and a percentage would put its release in
       the middle of the words. */
    --claim-glow: 62px;   /* the far bank is handed back above this */
    --claim-in:   96px;
    --claim-full: 168px;  /* full weight before the first cap-height */
    --claim-mid:  300px;
    --claim-hold: 500px;  /* full weight past the last baseline */
    --claim-out:  540px;  /* release begins below the last descender */
    --claim-fade: 580px;
    --claim-end:  620px;  /* the river is the river again from here down */
    background: linear-gradient(180deg,
      transparent 0px,
      transparent var(--claim-glow),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 16%, transparent) var(--claim-in),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 44%, transparent) var(--claim-full),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 46%, transparent) var(--claim-mid),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 44%, transparent) var(--claim-hold),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 32%, transparent) var(--claim-out),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 8%, transparent) var(--claim-fade),
      transparent var(--claim-end));
  }

  /* ── Register 2: the poster ───────────────────────────────────────
     Sits at the TOP of the open band, not centred in it, so the photograph
     keeps the middle-right of the frame. */
  /* The shelf under the fixed bar. It used to be produced by the caption
     rail's own padding one row above; the caption is Figure's now, so the
     poster carries the offset itself — one value, in one place, so the hero
     cannot drift away from the shelf every inner page starts on. */
  .hero__mid {
    width: 100%;
    align-self: start;
    padding-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + clamp(1.8rem, 5.4vh, 3.4rem));
  }
  .hero__h { margin: 0; font-weight: inherit; }

  /* THE BRIDGE. The rung between the 11.7px rail and the 94px claim; it is
     now the shared .bridge from base.css, so the site has one bridge voice
     and not two. Only the block behaviour and the load stage are local. */
  .hero__kicker {
    display: block;
    /* Raised one rung, 26.6 → 33.8, via the shared .bridge--lift: the jump
       from clause to claim was 3.5x while every other transition on the page
       is under 2x. The ladder here is 11.7 rail → 33.8 clause → 93 claim,
       i.e. 2.9x then 2.8x. The size lives in base.css so this is not a third
       hand-rolled sans display rung. */
    /* ONE LINE, wherever one line fits. The measure was 26ch, which is 4ch
       short of the clause, so the front door opened on a 377px line, a 243px
       stub, and then 969 / 1096 / 1263px of display type. A four-step block
       whose second step is its shortest reads as a wrapping accident, which
       is what it was. At 34ch the clause sets on one line at 629px and the
       block becomes a single staircase — 629 / 969 / 1096 / 1263 — every
       step longer than the one above it, opening to the right the same way
       the light in the photograph does. It also gives the claim back the
       44px the stub was holding, which is air over the subject. */
    max-width: min(34ch, 100%);
    margin-bottom: clamp(0.55rem, 1.2vh, 0.95rem);
    /* Optical inset — see the note on .hero__poster. Libre Franklin's cap T
       carries 1.31px of left sidebearing at 33.79px; the em value is the
       bearing over the em, so it holds at every rung of the clamp. */
    margin-left: -0.039em;
    opacity: 0;
    animation: heroIn 720ms var(--ease-out) 220ms forwards;
  }

  /* Nine words, and the measure is the frame's until 10.6em is narrower. */
  .hero__poster {
    display: block;
    /* --step-banner. The token's whole job is to be spent ONCE per site, on
       the hero, and it was being spent twice on inner pages and never here:
       the front door ran a local 5.9rem clamp and was out-shouted by 35% by
       two secondary pages. It is the banner now, at 126.2px on a 1440 frame.

       THE RAG IS THE RAG. This used to claim that 10.6em fixed the line
       breaks "identical at every viewport width" and landed the claim
       "corner-to-corner across the frame". Measured, neither is true and
       neither can be: the claim sets three lines above 1081px, four between
       1081 and ~430, five below, and on a 1440 frame its longest line ends
       at 1263px inside a 1338px measure — 75px short, because that is what
       the words are, and nothing short of retracking a 126px serif to zero
       would close it. So it is left as a rag and composed as one. With the
       clause on a single line (see .hero__kicker) the block is

           627 · 969 · 1093 · 1263

       four steps, each longer than the one above it, opening to the right
       the way the light on this river does. A staircase is a rag a reader
       can see the reason for; the previous 377 / 243 / 969 / 1093 / 1263
       was not. */
    font-size: var(--step-banner);
    line-height: 0.93;
    letter-spacing: -0.028em;
    /* The 0.09em is a RIGHT BEARING, and it is there to kill one width.
       Both terms of this constraint scale with the viewport — the shell is
       0.942w - 19.2 and the third line is 168.1 + 0.7605w — so they cross,
       once, at w = 1032. Without a reserved bearing the claim therefore sets
       three lines that end 1.5px from the frame at 1035 and 4px at 1045: a
       126px serif with no right sidebearing against the edge of the page,
       which reads as a mistake rather than a measure. Reserving 0.09em moves
       the crossing to 1081 and guarantees the last line at least 9px of air
       at every width above it, opening to 75px at 1440. Below 1081 the claim
       sets four lines and ends on "today." alone, which is the emphatic word
       of the sentence and can carry a line. */
    max-width: min(10.6em, calc(100% - 0.09em));
    text-wrap: initial;
    hyphens: none;

    /* OPTICAL INSET. Measured on the render at deviceScaleFactor 3, first
       column reaching half of the line's peak ink, against a layout rail of
       51.36px:

         hero rule        51.00   -0.36    the rail everything answers to
         index "01"       52.00   +0.64
         eyebrow "F"      52.33   +0.97
         kicker "T"       52.67   +1.31
         poster "A"       51.00   -0.36    line 3
         poster "t"       53.67   +2.31    lines 1 and 2

       So the largest object on the site stood 2.3px inboard of the hairline
       drawn beneath it — and 2.3px inboard of the "A" in its own third line,
       which is the part a reader actually sees: one left edge, ragged by
       2.3px, at 126px. Newsreader's "t" carries that as sidebearing; "A" has
       none, because a diagonal is meant to hang. Aligning the STEMS puts the
       t on the rail and lets the A overhang by the same 2.3px, which is what
       optical margin alignment is. In em, so it holds from 46px to 134px. */
    margin-left: -0.018em;

    /* The claim is inked in, top down, so the hero has a hierarchy in time as
       well as in size: rail, clause, claim, ledger. A soft-edged mask rather
       than the house .lines reveal — .lines is split on fonts.ready and marked
       in-view by an observer that has already fired at the top of the page, so
       whichever wins the race decides whether the hero animates at all. This
       is load-timed and therefore always the same. Local to this component.

       THE MASK BOX WAS NOT THE INK BOX, and the biggest glyph on the site
       paid for it. A mask is clipped to `mask-clip`, which defaults to the
       BORDER BOX: every pixel of ink outside that box is dropped before the
       mask image is even sampled — permanently, in the finished state as
       much as during the wipe. And the ink here does leave the box, because
       the display line-height (0.93) is SMALLER than the font-size. The
       half-leading is negative, so line 1's font box opens ABOVE the
       element's own top edge. Measured on the 1440 render, Newsreader at
       126.24px:

         element border box top   166.67
         line 1 font box top      161.67   (baseline 254.67, ascent 93)
         line 1 INK top           160.67   (actualBoundingBoxAscent 94)
                                  ------
         ink outside the box        6.00px  = 0.0475em

       Six pixels, on line 1 only — the line that carries "of". Its "f" lost
       the hook: cut flat, with the teardrop terminal left floating beside
       the stem as a detached wedge. The i-dots in "technologies" were sliced
       to bars and the h/l/t ascender tips were shaved. Lines 2 and 3 sit
       inside the box and were untouched, which is why the "f" of "families"
       two lines down was always perfect — the proof it was the box and never
       the font. line-height / font-size is a fixed 0.930 at every width, so
       this held at 1024, 1280, 1440 and 1728 alike.

       The fix is to make the border box contain the ink, NOT to enlarge the
       mask image: `mask-clip: no-clip` reads back correctly from
       getComputedStyle in Chrome 141 and still clips at the border box in
       paint, and growing `mask-size` past a box the mask is clipped to buys
       nothing. Verified by injection at 0.09 / 0.18 / 0.30 / 0.50em — the
       cut never moved off 166.67 — and by an opaque `linear-gradient(#000,
       #000)` at `100% 100%`, which severed the f just the same.

       So the box grows instead. `padding-block` pushes the border box past
       the ink at both ends and `margin-block` takes the same amount back:
       the two adjoining margins here are opposite in sign, so they collapse
       to their sum and the type does not move — verified, all three line
       boxes land on 161.67 / 279.06 / 396.45 before and after. The mask
       keeps its original position, size and 980ms travel; it simply now has
       the whole letterform to wipe.

       0.09em is ~1.9x the measured 6px overshoot — headroom for a taller
       accent or a fallback face — and at the foot it covers a descender that
       was clearing the old box by 0.39px. Horizontally nothing is needed:
       the "A" of "American" is set to hang by the optical inset above, and
       its ink lands ON the box edge, not outside it (checked against an
       unmasked render at 5x). */
    --ink-rise: 0.09em;
    padding-block: var(--ink-rise);
    margin-block: calc(-1 * var(--ink-rise));
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg, #000 0 58%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg, #000 0 58%, transparent 100%);
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat; mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    -webkit-mask-position: 0 0; mask-position: 0 0;
    -webkit-mask-size: 100% 0%; mask-size: 100% 0%;
    animation: posterInk 980ms var(--ease-out) 240ms forwards;
  }
  @keyframes posterInk { to { -webkit-mask-size: 100% 190%; mask-size: 100% 190%; } }

  /* ── Register 3: the 2026 ledger ──────────────────────────────────
     Three ranked sizes per cell. The partner name is the loud one. */
  .hero__index {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    padding-bottom: clamp(1.75rem, 4.4vh, 3.1rem);
  }
  /* THE LEDGER'S OWN GROUND — the half of the foot that Figure cannot place.
     Figure's ramp is a percentage of the FRAME, and the ledger is 14% of a
     1440x900 desktop frame but 28% of a 390x844 phone. One percentage cannot
     carry both: at `rise={0.30}` the desktop rows measure 4–17 L* and pass
     comfortably, while the top two phone rows measured 34 and 35, which put
     "Healthy Athletes" and "Clean Data Centers" at 4.0 and 4.2 : 1 — under
     the 4.5 the meta register needs, on the two most senior partners in the
     manifest.

     Rather than raise `rise` until the phone passes — which on the desktop
     would push the ramp back up over the man and the dog, the exact thing
     this round is here to give back — the shortfall is made up by a band
     anchored to the LEDGER. `top: -30%` is 30% of the ledger's own height,
     so the lead-in is always 30%, the first row always lands at 23% of the
     band and the last at 72%, on every viewport. One set of stops, no
     breakpoint. It reads as nothing on the desktop, where the print's own
     foot is already carrying the band and this only tops it up.

     Round 3 raised it by roughly a sixth — 36 → 42% at the eyebrow, 50 → 55%
     at the first row — because the crop that brought the pair up into the
     open band also brought the lit water down into the top of the manifest.
     On the phone the eyebrow and the first project title now sit on the
     brightest part of the photograph. Measured after: worst row 4.97 : 1 on
     the phone (project title, --fg-meta at 54%), 9.43 : 1 on the desktop,
     eyebrow 5.31 : 1 and 12.95 : 1. */
  .hero__index::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: -30% 0 0;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: linear-gradient(180deg,
      transparent 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 18%, transparent) 12%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 48%, transparent) 22%,   /* eyebrow */
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 64%, transparent) 34%,   /* row 1 */
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 68%, transparent) 60%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 70%, transparent) 100%);
  }
  .hero__index-head {
