  /* THE PHONE SETTING, COMPOSED.
     At 390 the claim wraps naturally to 343 / 183 / 194 / 328 / 109 px on a
     348px measure: the first line jammed to within 5px of the margin, then
     two single-word lines. Five lines of 46px serif is the right amount of
     voice on a phone; that rag is not.

     Holding "of tomorrow" together moves one word up and one word down and
     the rag becomes 295 / 230 / 194 / 328 / 109 — no line inside 20px of the
     margin, no orphan, and the block keeps a left column instead of running
     edge to edge. It is done with a nowrap group rather than a narrower
     max-width on purpose: a measure that sits 7px below "the technologies of"
     and 9px above "American families" is a knife edge that a font fallback or
     a different rasteriser flips. The group depends only on the ORDER of the
     word widths, which does not move. */
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .hero__poster { letter-spacing: -0.026em; }
    .hero__keep { white-space: nowrap; }
  }

  /* ── THE PHONE'S PHOTOGRAPHIC BAND ───────────────────────────────
     The phone is not a narrow desktop and this hero cannot be re-cropped
     into one. The negative is 2800x1400 and the man-and-dog silhouette
     occupies source y 875-1255 — 27% of the frame's HEIGHT. On a portrait
     cover box that fraction is locked: `object-fit: cover` scales the
     photograph to the box's height, so the pair renders at 0.27 x the box
     height NO MATTER what focal or zoom is asked for. At 390x844 the media
     box is 979px tall (844 + Figure's 8% parallax overscan), so the pair is
     never smaller than ~265px and at the shipped zoom it is 315px.

     The band it has to live in was 217px — headline bottom 390 to the
     ledger's eyebrow at 607. A 315px subject does not fit in 217px, which
     is why every crop tried in rounds 1-3 put either the headline or the
     manifest on top of the man. The crop was never the whole problem; the
     phone's vertical rhythm was.

     So the manifest is set at the phone's own pitch rather than the
     desktop's. Four rows at a 36px pitch instead of 43, a shorter foot, a
     tighter eyebrow — every value still above the 11px legibility floor and
     the rows still 36px of vertical target — and the eyebrow drops from 607
     to 646. The photographic band goes 217 -> 256px, which is what lets the
     crop below put the man's head 25px clear of "today." and the dog's head
     and the reaching arm wholly in open water. Nothing about the ledger's
     content, order or register changes; only its pitch. */
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .hero__index { padding-bottom: clamp(1.3rem, 3.1vh, 2.1rem); }
    .hero__index-head { margin-bottom: clamp(0.6rem, 1.3vh, 0.95rem); }
    .hcell { padding-block: clamp(0.42rem, 1.12vh, 0.62rem); }
  }

  /* Measured: the longest partner name and the longest project title stop
     sharing a line below ~390px. Rather than truncate a partner, the project
     drops to its own line — in every row, so the block is still four
     identical rows and not four ragged ones.

     The guard used to stop at 374px, which left a live band from 375 to
     ~385 where the fix was off and the truncation was on: at 375px --- the
     iPhone SE and iPhone 8, still the common small screen --- "Secretary
     Miguel Cardona" rendered as "Secretary Miguel Card...". A named public
     official is the credibility this page runs on; it is the one string on
     the site that must never be elided. Re-measured across 360-460: clean
     at 360 (guard on) and at 390+ (room), truncating by 5px at 375 and 2px
     at 380. The guard now covers the whole band. */
  @media (max-width: 389px) {
    .hcell__sub {
      grid-column: 2 / -1;
      grid-row: 2;
      margin-top: 0.42rem;
      /* …and it stops hugging the right wall when it does. The row above
         sets `justify-self: end` so the project can sit hard right on the
         SAME line as the partner; once it drops to a line of its own that
         alignment has nothing to answer to, and on a 375px screen it left
         "Special Olympics" flush left with "HEALTHY ATHLETES" flush right
         two rows below it — one row, two rags, reading as two entries. */
      justify-self: start;
    }
  }
